\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{sec:hyperparameters}

The framework introduces two hyperparameters, both with empirically robust defaults set once across all experiments.

\paragraph{Quality threshold $\tau$.}
Convergence theory for contaminated training shows that convergence rate improves with the effective real fraction~\citep{wang2026survive}, motivating a lower bound on $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$.
The specific value of $\tau$ is set empirically: it reduces the search from a multi-run grid (nine full training runs across $b \in [0.1, 5.0]$ with 0.17 PPL variation) to a single default value.
We find a wide safe range: $\tau \in [0.5, 0.8]$ consistently outperforms uniform training (Section~\ref{sec:results}), with $\tau = 0.8$ achieving the best perplexity and ${<}0.035$ PPL variation within the safe range.
Above 0.8, $b^{*}$ inflates rapidly (mean $b^{*} = 3.08$ at $\tau = 0.95$), causing over-downweighting.
We use $\tau = 0.8$ as the default throughout.

\paragraph{ESS floor $n_{\min}$.}
We recommend $n_{\min} = \max(2, \lfloor B/2 \rfloor)$ as a default that scales with batch size without manual tuning.
At this ratio, the ESS ceiling binds on a majority of batches (providing gradient stability) while leaving room for $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ to determine $b^{*}$ in the remainder (enabling quality-driven intervention).
Ratios below ${\sim}1/4$ effectively remove the stability constraint ($n_{\min}{=}1$, $B{=}4$: mean $b^{*}$ rises to 7.20, PPL degrades by $+$0.14), while $n_{\min}/B{=}1$ prevents intervention entirely ($b^{*} \equiv 0$, recovering uniform training).
This default is validated at $B \in \{4, 8, 16\}$ with PPL differences ${<}0.01$ (Section~\ref{sec:sensitivity}); we leave fine-grained ratio characterization (e.g., $0.4$ vs.\ $0.6$) to future work.
A practical caveat: ESS is computed per micro-batch (single forward pass), so $n_{\min}$ must satisfy $n_{\min} \leq B_{\mathrm{micro}}$; setting it relative to the effective batch (after gradient accumulation) silently forces $b^{*}{=}0$ on every step.

\section{Proofs}
\label{app:proofs}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:monotone}]
Let $w_i(b) = (1 - q_i)^b$, $W(b) = \sum_{j=1}^B w_j(b)$, and $\pi_i(b) = w_i(b) / W(b)$.
Then
\begin{equation*}
  \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) = \sum_{i=1}^{B} \pi_i(b)\, r_i,
\end{equation*}
where $r_i \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates whether sample $i$ is real.
Taking the derivative with respect to $b$:
\begin{align*}
  \frac{d\pi_i}{db}
  &= \pi_i \!\left(\frac{d \ln w_i}{db} - \frac{d \ln W}{db}\right)
  = \pi_i \!\left(\ln(1{-}q_i) - \sum_j \pi_j \ln(1{-}q_j)\right).
\end{align*}
Substituting,
\begin{align*}
  \frac{d\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}}{db}
  &= \sum_i r_i\, \pi_i \!\left(\ln(1{-}q_i) - \mathbb{E}_{\pi}[\ln(1{-}q)]\right) \\
  &= \mathrm{Cov}_{\pi}\!\bigl(r_i,\, \ln(1{-}q_i)\bigr).
\end{align*}
For any detector with better-than-random discrimination, real samples ($r_i = 1$) have $q_i \leq 0.5$ on average, and thus higher $\ln(1{-}q_i)$ than synthetic samples.
The weighted covariance $\mathrm{Cov}_{\pi}(r_i, \ln(1{-}q_i))$ is therefore non-negative, establishing that $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ is non-decreasing in $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[ESS monotonicity]
\label{prop:ess_mono}
$\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ is non-increasing in $b$, and strictly decreasing whenever the batch contains at least two samples with distinct $q$-values.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Write $\mathrm{ESS}(b) = 1 / \sum_i \pi_i(b)^2$, where $\pi_i(b) = w_i(b) / W(b)$.
The denominator $\sum_i \pi_i^2$ is the Herfindahl index $H$.
Differentiating $H$ with respect to $b$:
\begin{equation*}
  \frac{dH}{db} = 2 \sum_i \pi_i^2 \bigl(\ln(1{-}q_i) - \mathbb{E}_{\pi}[\ln(1{-}q)]\bigr).
\end{equation*}
This equals $2\,\mathrm{Var}_{\pi^2}[\ln(1{-}q)]$ under the $\pi^2$-weighted measure.
Since variance is non-negative and strictly positive when not all $\ln(1{-}q_i)$ coincide (i.e., when at least two $q$-values differ), $H$ is non-decreasing and $\mathrm{ESS} = 1/H$ is non-increasing.
This is a standard property of exponential tilting~\citep{kish1965survey,owen2013mc}.
\end{proof}

